Your investors have a point about speed and simplicity, but equity is the wrong money for this cost. Here is why (judgement throughout, with figures from stated assumptions).

**Working capital comes back; dilution does not.** Each project's cash returns at go-live, about 6.5 months later. Equity spent on that cycle buys a cost that repays itself, and you give up shares for it permanently.

**An illustration.** Say you raise an extra €4m to cover the cash cycle, at a €48m post-money valuation (my assumption). That is about 8% more of the company, for good. At a €150m exit, it is worth about €12m. A €4m guaranteed line might cost 8–10% a year all-in, interest plus EIFO's premium of 0.8–6.3% [c4]: roughly €350k a year. You pay that only while you draw on the line.

**It grows with you.** At 25 projects a year you need about €7.5m out at once. With equity you would be raising again at every growth step. A guaranteed line can grow with the order book.

**Your investors gain too.** The less equity goes into the cash cycle, the less everyone is diluted. The round also makes the bank route work, because the guarantee needs positive equity [c2] and new lending [c9]. So these are one move, not two.

**Where I'd meet them:** add a modest buffer to the round, not a working-capital fund.

