Leasing is worth having as a sales option, but it won't fix your cash problem (judgement throughout).

**It moves the cost; it doesn't remove it.** Every customer would need approval from a leasing company, and that adds a step and time to each sale. The customer also pays the lessor's margin on top of your price. Your offer gets dearer and slower, at the very moment you want to scale.

**Your cash gap may not move.** Leasing companies normally pay the supplier when the customer accepts the installation. For you, acceptance is go-live, so the 4–9 months of pre-financing could stay with you. Ask a lessor exactly when they pay you before counting on it.

**The risk tends to come back.** Supplier leasing programmes often ask the supplier to buy the kit back or share losses. Your accountant should confirm whether that keeps the kit on your balance sheet anyway.

**Where it does fit:** later, as an option for customers who want monthly payments. For German customers buying from the Danish company, EIFO lists an operational leasing guarantee under its buyer-financing products [corpus row, unverified]. That is a complement to sales, not the source of your working capital.

The stronger story is the one public funders exist for. Lenders misprice a company that carries the project cost until go-live. A public guarantee fixes that, so your capital recycles faster and the pipeline grows.

